\name{pva_state}
\alias{pva_state}
\alias{pva_population}
\alias{add_individuals}
\title{Population state}
\usage{
pva_population(sex, age, ..., mother_id = NA_integer_, father_id = NA_integer_)

pva_state(
  population,
  globals = list(),
  pedigree = FALSE,
  kinship = NULL,
  max_dense_kinship = 2000L
)

add_individuals(
  state,
  sex,
  age,
  ...,
  mother_id = NA_integer_,
  father_id = NA_integer_
)
}
\arguments{
\item{sex}{Character vector of \code{"F"} and \code{"M"}, one per individual.}
\item{age}{Whole-number ages (length one, or one per individual).}
\item{\dots}{Additional named fields: one value, or one per individual. For \code{add_individuals()} they must be exactly the model's non-core fields.}
\item{mother_id}{Mother identifiers, with \code{NA} for an unknown mother.}
\item{father_id}{Father identifiers, with \code{NA} for an unknown father.}
\item{population}{A population from \code{pva_population()}, or a data frame or named list with at least \code{sex} and \code{age}.}
\item{globals}{Named list of shared dynamic values, such as the year's environmental deviates.}
\item{pedigree}{Whether the engine tracks exact pedigree inbreeding. When \code{TRUE} the population gains the engine-owned field \code{pedigree_F}.}
\item{kinship}{Optional matrix of starting coancestries in population order. It takes precedence over recorded initial parentage.}
\item{max_dense_kinship}{Maximum number of dense pedigree slots per replicate (default 2000). Once the required slots exceed this limit, the engine uses exact sparse ancestor tracing for the rest of that replicate, even if the population later falls below the limit.}
\item{state}{A \code{pva_state}.}
}
\description{
The population is columnar: a named list of equal-length vectors with one element per \emph{living} individual. \code{pva_population()} builds it, \code{pva_state()} wraps it with shared globals and the identifier allocator, and \code{add_individuals()} is the only way for a process to create individuals.
}
\details{
Core fields are \code{id}, \code{sex}, \code{age}, \code{alive}, \code{mother_id}, \code{father_id}, \code{origin} and \code{entry_time}. The engine owns \code{id}, \code{origin} (\code{"initial"}, \code{"birth"} or \code{"introduction"}), \code{entry_time} (the interval of entry, \code{-1} for the initial population) and \code{pedigree_F}; processes cannot write them. A process records mortality by setting \code{alive} to \code{FALSE}. After each process the engine moves those records out of the living table into the optional ledger, so every process starts with living individuals only.

Without a supplied matrix, initial parent IDs must resolve inside the initial population in an acyclic pedigree. Each unknown parent is an unrelated, non-inbred contribution. A newborn's inbreeding coefficient is its parents' kinship. Known parents can be living or can have died earlier in the same interval. Use \code{\link{kinship_matrix}} for living pairs in either storage mode.
}
\value{
\code{pva_population()} returns the columnar population. \code{pva_state()} returns a \code{pva_state} with elements \code{population}, \code{globals}, \code{next_id}, \code{kinship} and \code{max_dense_kinship}. \code{add_individuals()} returns the state with the new records appended and \code{next_id} advanced.
}
\examples{
population <- pva_population(sex = c("F", "F", "M"), age = c(3, 1, 4), patch_id = 1L)
state <- pva_state(population, globals = list(year_quality = 1), pedigree = TRUE)
state
state <- add_individuals(state, sex = "F", age = 0L, patch_id = 1L,
                         mother_id = 1L, father_id = 3L)
length(state$population$id)
}
